{
  "concepts": [
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "An operational engineering profile for preferred realisation structure and design discipline. Its engineering Ontology concerns one designed boundary; using the profile does not require adopting a world-model, Constraint-Emergence Ontology, or metaphysical or empirical programme.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1691,
          "quote":"This profile is operational and stands on its own.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE.md#DESIGN_MODULE%2F8.%20Theoretical%20framing",
          "start_line":1686
        }
      ],
      "key":"design_profile",
      "label":"Design-module profile"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"The three domain views show domain structure, execution and lifecycle. A computational realisation projection additionally exposes abstract data types, identity and key structures, algorithms, algebraic laws, mutation and effect boundaries, selected implementations and domain-to-computational mappings, including repeated machinery obscured by domain names.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1619,
          "quote":"domain design into its computational realisation: abstract data types,\nidentity and key structures, algorithms, algebraic laws, mutation and\neffect boundaries, selected implementations, and domain-to-computational\nmappings.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE.md#DESIGN_MODULE%2F7.%20Ontology%20and%20three-view%20design%20gate%2FComputational%20realisation%20projection%20%28UP-019%29",
          "start_line":1616
        }
      ],
      "key":"computational_projection",
      "label":"Computational realisation projection"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"The explicit semantic and operational roles declared for a material realisation component. The declared role set is design authority; technological capability creates no authority.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1603,
          "quote":"A component's declared role set is design authority;",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE.md#DESIGN_MODULE%2F7.%20Ontology%20and%20three-view%20design%20gate%2FComputational%20realisation%20projection%20%28UP-019%29",
          "start_line":1603
        }
      ],
      "key":"component_role_set",
      "label":"Component role set"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Optional theoretical framing in which a Markov object is a candidate identity-bearing cut: a stable self-bounding pattern with an effective blanket separating identity-bearing from contextual structure. IACS analogises the smallest such cut; the Promotion Test asks whether a payload has independent boundary authority; Boundary Inflation mistakes fragments for peer objects; semantic-boundary strong typing keeps the cut explicit.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1697,
          "quote":"It treats a Markov object\nas a candidate identity-bearing cut: a stable self-bounding pattern whose\neffective blanket separates what is load-bearing for identity from what is\nload-bearing for context.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE.md#DESIGN_MODULE%2F8.%20Theoretical%20framing",
          "start_line":1694
        }
      ],
      "key":"markov_object_framing",
      "label":"Markov-object theoretical framing"
    },
    {
      "conditions":["The profile governs the realisation boundary."],
      "definition":"Recommended module roles: carrier modules define typed immutable inputs, outputs, contracts, transitions, records or envelopes; semantic kernels transform carriers without effects; effect shells perform emission, publication, network, subprocess, persistence or delivery effects; projections derive read models or reports from authoritative truth; bindings/adapters translate external boundaries to internal carriers without inventing law; constructors/materialisers write generated or built artifacts from admitted carriers and explicit plans. Module boundaries constrain traversal reasoning under finite context, attention and time, expose ownership, consumption, emissions and sufficient proof, and make recurrence visible for library consolidation.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1732,
          "quote":"- constructor or materialisation module: writes generated or built\n  artifacts from admitted carriers and explicit plans.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE.md#DESIGN_MODULE%2F9.%20Module%20taxonomy",
          "start_line":1731
        }
      ],
      "key":"module_taxonomy",
      "label":"Module role taxonomy"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Typed values carrying source truth, preferably produced by a constructor or parser, consumed by transforms that return another carrier or closed result, and read by projections without mutation.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1769,
          "quote":"preferred shape: a constructor or parser produces a typed carrier;\ntransforms consume one carrier and return another carrier or a closed\nresult; projections read those carriers without mutating them.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE.md#DESIGN_MODULE%2F10.%20Realisation%20rules%2FImmutable%20carriers",
          "start_line":1767
        }
      ],
      "key":"truth_carrier",
      "label":"Typed truth carrier"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"A Product semantic function may encounter input outside its admitted domain. Its returned outcomes are Product domain values, distinct from the method's core results; an evaluation consuming those outcomes returns its own core result.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1785,
          "quote":"The outcome values a\nsemantic function returns are the Product's domain values, not results of\nthis method; an evaluation that consumes them returns its own core value",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE.md#DESIGN_MODULE%2F10.%20Realisation%20rules%2FTotality",
          "start_line":1782
        }
      ],
      "key":"semantic_function",
      "label":"Semantic function and Product outcomes"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"An explicit boundary for effects such as file writes, network calls, subprocess execution, event append, manifest publication, prompt delivery and database mutation.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1799,
          "quote":"Typical effect edges include file writes, network calls, subprocess\nexecution, event append, manifest publication, prompt delivery and\ndatabase mutation.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE.md#DESIGN_MODULE%2F10.%20Realisation%20rules%2FEffect%20edge",
          "start_line":1797
        }
      ],
      "key":"effect_edge",
      "label":"Effect edge"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"A hidden semantic centre is an orchestration or wrapper surface that procedurally holds meaning which should already reside in carriers, contracts or explicit design modules. Its diagnostic is needing to read that module to discover closure, identity, failure, next work or current truth.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1816,
          "quote":"A\nmodule is acting as an unlawful semantic centre when reviewers have to\nread it to discover what counts as closure, what identity means, what\nfailure means, what work is next, or what the current truth is.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE.md#DESIGN_MODULE%2F10.%20Realisation%20rules%2FNo%20semantic%20centre",
          "start_line":1813
        }
      ],
      "key":"hidden_semantic_centre",
      "label":"Hidden semantic centre"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"A boundary governing, observing, routing, auditing or evaluating another actor, executor, builder or substrate. Governance surfaces describe facts, state, boundary and admissible routes; strategy surfaces prescribe how another actor should work.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1848,
          "quote":"Governance surfaces describe the state, boundary\nand admissible routes; strategy surfaces prescribe how another actor\nshould do the work.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE.md#DESIGN_MODULE%2F10.%20Realisation%20rules%2FGovernance%20and%20strategy%20separation",
          "start_line":1846
        }
      ],
      "key":"governance_boundary",
      "label":"Governance and strategy surfaces"
    },
    {
      "conditions":["A realisation boundary is lawfully open for migration, refactor or bounded implementation work."],
      "definition":"Opportunistic optimisation is local improvement subordinate to constitutional truth, declared module ownership evidence and functional equivalence. Cross-boundary improvements are repricing inputs rather than silently enlarged clean-up.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1860,
          "quote":"This does not\noutrank constitutional truth, declared module ownership evidence or\nfunctional equivalence; it is subordinate to them.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE.md#DESIGN_MODULE%2F10.%20Realisation%20rules%2FOpportunistic%20optimisation",
          "start_line":1857
        }
      ],
      "key":"opportunistic_optimisation",
      "label":"Opportunistic optimisation"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Review of repeated realisation machinery as a commonisation candidate. Typical candidates are authority-neutral, not Product truth, reused across two or more tickets or boundaries, and locally adaptable without changing semantic ownership: expectation derivation, nested contract or policy carriers, proof helpers, fixtures, effect plans and constructors that own no semantic law.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1897,
          "quote":"When the same realisation pattern appears across more than one processed\nboundary, review treats that pattern as a commonisation candidate rather\nthan as another purely local clean-up",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE.md#DESIGN_MODULE%2F10.%20Realisation%20rules%2FRecurrence%20extraction",
          "start_line":1895
        }
      ],
      "key":"recurrence_review",
      "label":"Recurrence extraction and commonisation"
    },
    {
      "conditions":["A ticket applies the profile to a boundary and claims that boundary has been processed."],
      "definition":"A bounded design check against the accepted design basis and exact closure claim. Its complete selected function is the claim's causal closure, not the whole Product or construction history; it reconstructs governing requirements through Ontology, Prime atoms, IACS, views, target modules, implementation and module-owned proof.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1965,
          "quote":"The complete selected function is the causal closure of the declared\nclaim, not the whole Product or the whole history of its construction.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE.md#DESIGN_MODULE%2F10.%20Realisation%20rules%2FPost-ticket%20design%20review",
          "start_line":1964
        }
      ],
      "key":"post_ticket_design_check",
      "label":"Post-ticket bounded design check"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"An interface family has one authoritative source truth consumed downstream. Interface bleed includes reconstructing another family's truth, projection becoming closure, reports/prompts becoming admission, wrappers deciding kernel semantics, or adapters inventing meaning.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":2028,
          "quote":"The required shape: each interface family has\none authoritative source truth; downstream interfaces consume that truth;\ncrossing an interface boundary does not create a new semantic centre.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE.md#DESIGN_MODULE%2F10.%20Realisation%20rules%2FInterface%20bleed%20prohibition",
          "start_line":2026
        }
      ],
      "key":"interface_family",
      "label":"Interface family and semantic bleed"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"A proxy interface is a new-looking surface that forwards to old authority, reconstructs missing truth from it, keeps it executable under a new name, or leaves proofs green while old law governs. Such substitution is bridge debt, distinct from explicitly retained compatibility.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":2039,
          "quote":"A proxy interface is a new-looking surface that\nforwards to the old authority path, reconstructs missing truth from the\nold path, keeps the old path executable behind a new name, or allows\nproofs to stay green while the old law still governs.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE.md#DESIGN_MODULE%2F10.%20Realisation%20rules%2FProxy%20interface%20prohibition",
          "start_line":2036
        }
      ],
      "key":"proxy_interface",
      "label":"Proxy interface and retained compatibility"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Review under the design profile includes provenance-bearing prompts, design-review questions, and an explicit functional-realisation checklist when functional bias, pure transforms, immutable carriers, single-owner truth or low coupling is claimed.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":2051,
          "quote":"A prompt clause that exists to enforce design law\nneeds provenance and a proof that the clause still causes or prevents the\nintended behaviour",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE.md#DESIGN_MODULE%2F11.%20Review",
          "start_line":2049
        },
        {
          "end_line":2214,
          "quote":"When a project claims functional bias, pure transforms, immutable\ncarriers, single-owner truth or low-coupling realisation, review\nexplicitly checks",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE.md#DESIGN_MODULE%2F11.%20Review%2FFunctional%20realisation%20checklist",
          "start_line":2212
        }
      ],
      "key":"design_review",
      "label":"Design review and functional checklist"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Product authority's adoption of an available design profile for a named boundary and, for partial adoption, a named rule family. Inclusion in the selected method cut makes the profile available but does not adopt it or create another adopter.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":2373,
          "quote":"inclusion in the selected method cut makes the profile available, and only\nProduct authority adopts it. This profile adds no adopter.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE.md#DESIGN_MODULE%2F12.%20Adoption%20and%20relations%2FAdoption",
          "start_line":2372
        }
      ],
      "key":"profile_adoption",
      "label":"Product adoption of the profile"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"The source's allocation of member responsibilities: SPEC_METHOD owns constitutional process, authority flow, repricing and migration; RELEASE owns release cut, Product, install, qualification and version-line promotion evaluation; ODD owns graph-native constructive carrier law; DESIGN_MODULE owns preferred implementation/design module structure; UX adds rendering, state-transition, effect-membrane and AssetSurface-binding discipline for UX surfaces. ASSURANCE module lifecycle confirmation detects sufficient lifecycle signal for building and review, not release or rollout eligibility.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":2419,
          "quote":"the UX profile ([UX](UX.md)) refines this profile for UX\nsurfaces with rendering, state-transition and effect-membrane rules and is\nloaded only when the work realises a UX surface",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE.md#DESIGN_MODULE%2F12.%20Adoption%20and%20relations%2FRelationship%20to%20other%20members",
          "start_line":2417
        }
      ],
      "key":"member_responsibility_allocation",
      "label":"Responsibilities of related members"
    },
    {
      "conditions":["A runtime realisation is consumer-bound to the ABG role under the graph-execution profile."],
      "definition":"A bound ABG runtime realisation may publish default identity generators, validators and normalisers for its own runtime surfaces and default ODD surface kinds used by default F_D helpers.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":2450,
          "quote":"the permission is scoped to that role\nand does not extend to other runtime realisations",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE.md#DESIGN_MODULE%2F12.%20Adoption%20and%20relations%2FIdentity%20defaults%20from%20a%20bound%20runtime%20realisation",
          "start_line":2449
        }
      ],
      "key":"bound_runtime_identity_defaults",
      "label":"Role-scoped runtime identity defaults"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"A Tier 1 member owning justification and acceptance of claims, evidence, independent assessment, review results, acceptance, realisation conformance and the equivalence ladder, frame and method qualification, and actual-use evidence of adequate attention. It consumes FOUNDATIONS' core results and defines no result values itself.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":9,
          "quote":"[Results](FOUNDATIONS.md#results) own the core result values this member\nuses; it defines no result values of its own.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION",
          "start_line":8
        }
      ],
      "key":"evaluation_member",
      "label":"EVALUATION member"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"A load-bearing assertion over an exact subject, property and governing basis, with a declared witness relation, excluded nearest weaker property and falsifier. Construction claims about recursive or repeated work distinguish preservation, progress, termination and outcome satisfaction.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":15,
          "quote":"A load-bearing claim names its exact subject, the property claimed, the\ngoverning basis, the relation between witness and claim, the nearest weaker\nproperty it excludes, and its falsifier, before a witness is selected.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F1.%20Claims",
          "start_line":13
        }
      ],
      "key":"load_bearing_claim",
      "label":"Load-bearing claim"
    },
    {
      "conditions":["Exact-subject claims are relied on for promotion, closure or release.","Evidence is finite and may observe a weaker subject, another subject or another basis.","A constructor may be probabilistic or otherwise fallible.","Each reliance boundary has a decision owner."],
      "definition":"F6 is the claims, evidence, assessment and acceptance family covering EVALUATION §§1–5. Its instance premises are conditions of governed work, not commitments or consequences of finite capacity alone. Its table traces consequences, rule owners, added policy/domain choices and consumers. P1, P2, P3 and P6 are cited as independent; P0 is recovered with named candidate-output or non-reacquirable-fact premises; P5 is derived from P0 and P6 with the open-world premise, and P4b from P0 and P2 with the step-produced-fact premise.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":71,
          "quote":"They are conditions of the work\nthis family governs, not commitments, and do not follow from finite\ncapacity alone.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F1.%20Claims%2FFoundation%20to%20law%3A%20claims%2C%20evidence%2C%20assessment%20and%20acceptance",
          "start_line":69
        },
        {
          "end_line":88,
          "quote":"P5 (derived from P0 and P6, with P0's open-world premise)",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F1.%20Claims%2FFoundation%20to%20law%3A%20claims%2C%20evidence%2C%20assessment%20and%20acceptance",
          "start_line":88
        },
        {
          "end_line":94,
          "quote":"P4b (derived from P0 and P2, with P0's premise that step-produced facts are not re-acquirable)",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F1.%20Claims%2FFoundation%20to%20law%3A%20claims%2C%20evidence%2C%20assessment%20and%20acceptance",
          "start_line":94
        }
      ],
      "key":"claims_foundation_family",
      "label":"Foundation-to-law family F6"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Three distinct bases: semantic basis comprises constitutional and accepted-design relations defining claim meaning; evidence basis comprises exact artifacts, events, logs, tests and observations; state projection is a current view derived from admitted semantic and evidence bases. Four distinct roles define law, supply observations, apply the declared evaluation relation and dispose by decision authority. Structural conformance concerns closed syntax, types, identities, references, bases and mechanically decidable predicates; semantic fidelity concerns meaning and materiality.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":136,
          "quote":"Semantic law defines what an evaluation means;\nevidence supplies observations; the evaluator applies the declared\nrelation; decision authority disposes.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F2.%20Separation",
          "start_line":134
        }
      ],
      "key":"evaluation_separations",
      "label":"Bases, roles and structural/semantic separation"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Assessment independent in authorship and evidence conditions. Constructor self-review remains construction output. Author identity is the party responsible for realised content; actor identity identifies whoever performs an operation and is not authority. Independence is not established by a fresh context or assumed across actor families.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":213,
          "quote":"Authorship is one of the conditions on\nwhich the independence of an assessment rests; the evidence conditions are\nthe other",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F3.%20Independent%20assessment",
          "start_line":211
        }
      ],
      "key":"independent_assessment",
      "label":"Independent assessment"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"A review returns one FOUNDATIONS core result per declared predicate actually evaluated, accompanied by exact subject and basis, consulted evidence, consequence-ranked findings with evidence and smallest bounded correction, residual uncertainty and invalidation conditions. A planned but unperformed predicate has no result, unlike an evaluated undecided predicate or required coverage with no planned assessment.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":254,
          "quote":"A declared predicate the review planned and did\nnot evaluate has no result (FOUNDATIONS §4, Results). The review names it\nas not performed and reports no core value for it, neither\n`indeterminate` nor `falsified`, so that conjunction keeps it apart from\nan undecided predicate and from required coverage that no planned\nassessment addresses",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F4.%20Review%20results",
          "start_line":249
        }
      ],
      "key":"review_result",
      "label":"Review result and absence of evaluation"
    },
    {
      "conditions":["A review returns findings with technical triage."],
      "definition":"Finding-level, evidence-bound assessment against the exact evaluated claim and basis. Severity is the assessor's consequence grade; priority, current promotion-boundary effect and disposition belong to the decision owner. Triage payload classes complete, indeterminate and not_applicable are neither core results nor dispositions, priorities or lifecycle states.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":282,
          "quote":"The payload classes `complete`,\n`indeterminate` and `not_applicable` classify the triage only; they are\nnot new results, dispositions, priorities or lifecycle states",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F4.%20Review%20results%2FResult%20and%20triage%20projection",
          "start_line":280
        }
      ],
      "key":"technical_triage",
      "label":"Technical triage projection"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Acceptance is the decision owner's disposition over an unchanged subject, bounded to its named instance and interval. Work-item closure is satisfaction of the item's stated closing condition; review is an input rather than closure.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":315,
          "quote":"A work item closes by the condition it stated, not by a reviewer's approval;\na reviewer result is an input to closure, never the closure. Green labels,\ndocument counts, review counts and historical outcomes are not acceptance.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-common/35fb4824a064a84df5af214a0d0e2da597c18ac6/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F5.%20Acceptance",
          "start_line":313
        }
      ],
      "key":"acceptance",
      "label":"Acceptance and work-item closure"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"A finding is a reported defect against the exact evaluated claim. Blocking current promotion is the decision owner's act over that finding. EVALUATION §5 supplies the shared blocking conditions; profiles and work carriers add only domain triggers.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":340,
          "quote":"This section is the\none statement of when a finding can block; profiles and work carriers\nrefer to it and add only their domain triggers.",
